# Noony — Type Safety Rules

## The Type Chain

Every Noony handler has a two-parameter type chain that MUST flow through every layer:

```
Handler<TBody, TUser>
  └─ .use(new MyMiddleware<TBody, TUser>())
       └─ implements BaseMiddleware<TBody, TUser>
            └─ before(context: Context<TBody, TUser>)
            └─ after(context: Context<TBody, TUser>)
```

If any layer drops the generics, the types degrade to `unknown` downstream.

## Two Approaches — Pick One Per Handler

### Option A: Explicit generics

```typescript
interface RequestBody { name: string; }
interface AuthUser { uid: string; role: string; }

const handler = new Handler<RequestBody, AuthUser>()
  .use(new ErrorHandlerMiddleware<RequestBody, AuthUser>())
  .use(new BodyValidationMiddleware<RequestBody, AuthUser>(schema))
  .handle(async (context: Context<RequestBody, AuthUser>) => {
    const { name } = context.req.validatedBody; // typed as RequestBody
    const { uid } = context.user;               // typed as AuthUser
  });
```

### Option B: Type inference via `createTypedHandler()`

```typescript
const handler = createTypedHandler(
  async (context: Context<RequestBody, AuthUser>) => {
    // types inferred from the controller annotation
  }
);
```

Do NOT mix both approaches in the same handler chain.

## Custom Middleware Type Rules

```typescript
// WRONG — breaks the type chain downstream
class MyMiddleware implements BaseMiddleware {
  async before(context: Context) { ... }
}

// CORRECT — generics preserved
class MyMiddleware<TBody = unknown, TUser = unknown>
  implements BaseMiddleware<TBody, TUser> {
  async before(context: Context<TBody, TUser>) { ... }
}
```

## Rules

- `as any` anywhere in the handler chain → types are broken; find the missing generic
- `BaseMiddleware` without `<TBody, TUser>` → type chain broken at that middleware
- `context.req.validatedBody` typed as `unknown` → Handler or BodyValidationMiddleware missing generics
- `context.user` typed as `unknown` → Handler missing `TUser` or auth middleware not typed
- Do NOT use `Handler<unknown>` with body validation — `validatedBody` stays `unknown`
- Do NOT cast `Handler<any, any>` — bypasses all checking

## Forbidden Patterns

```typescript
// All forbidden:
as any
as unknown as SomeType
Handler<unknown, unknown>  // when you have actual types
(context as any).req.validatedBody
```

